> If "djuno" doesn't involve knowledge, then what does it involve?

It does involve knowledge. But the important thing is that we
have agreed that it involves the thing that English word "knowledge"
denotes. Likewise we are agreed that "gerku" = "dog" and "xekri"
= "black". Going on to debate the nature of knowledge, dogginess
and black is interesting, but in itself not essential for Lojban.

> I haven't even introduced, he said with a malicious grin, Nozick's
> four-point definition of knowledge, viz:
>
>         A knows p iff:
>                 1) A believes p;
>                 2) p is true;
>                 3) if p were false, then A wouldn't believe it;
>                 4) if p were true, then A would believe it.

That's very helpful.
It should be noted that the statement can't be interpreted with
purely logical "if"s. There is also implicit quantification over
possible worlds. In that case, (3) makes sense.
(4) interpreted with logical "if" is vacuous. Interpreted with
conditional "if", I don't think it's sufficient in itself; it
must at least be understood as "In all possible worlds in which
p is true and A entertains p, A believes p".
Maybe 3-4 could be combined as "In all possible worlds,
p is true and A entertains p iff A believes p".

